1. SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS

 In order to work on ccdb development and Cray ccdb repository maintenance
 the following third party tools are required:

  - GNU autoconf v2.66 or Newer
  - GNU automake v1.11 or Newer
  - GNU m4 1.4.14 or Newer
  - GNU make
  - GNU GCC

 It is recommended that the developer compile the source while using the Cray 
 supported PrgEnv-gnu module. It is also recommended that the developer use 
 their own local install of the GNU autotools suite (i.e. autoconf, automake, m4).

 If the developer does not wish to maintain their own install of the autotools
 suite, they can prefix their path with the css location of:

    /cray/css/u6/andrewg/usr/bin

 At the time of this writing, the above location has an install of the newer
 autotools suite which meets the minimum above requirements. This install is
 maintained by Andrew Gontarek of the PE Debugger Development group. Any
 questions should be directed at his email andrewg@cray.com.

2. Making Changes to the Source

 The developer that is actively working on the ccdb tool who wishes to
 make changes to any of the source files inside of the guard and libaif
 directories in this repository should do so only within a local working 
 copy of the Monash source repository. The Cray repository is meant to
 be a local mirror of the Monash source repository with additional Cray
 only material relating to building, modulefiles, and documentation.

 As this is a joint project with Guardsoft Ltd. and Monash University, 
 any non-Cray specific code changes (i.e. any changes relating to the 
 guard/ and libaif/ directories) should be shared with our partners.

 If you do not have access to the Monash source repository and would like
 to propose a bugfix for ccdb, you can do so by contacting a member of the
 PE Debugger Development group. Contact information can be found inside of
 the README document.

3. Auto-Merging Changes

 Since the Cray ccdb repository is a superset mirror of the Monash guard source
 repository, there needs to be a mechanism to synchronize the two together.
 Monash is using an older version of the SVN server (1.3.1 at the time of this
 writing) so using the svnsync tool is out of the question. The only mechanism
 that exists is to use the `svn merge` command in order to pull in the changes
 from monash into a local working copy of the Cray ccdb repository.

 To assit in facilitating this process, a helper script located inside of the
 'scripts' directory named 'merge_monash.sh' has been included for your use.
 To use the script, you should first make sure that you have write access to
 your current $PWD, then you can invoke the script directly. 

 As an example, lets say I have a local working copy of the Cray repository
 inside of my css drive that I have write access to. I then cd to the
 scripts/ directory of my local working copy and issue the following command:

  $ ./merge_monash.sh

 As long as you have your SVN credentials for both the Cray svn repository and
 the Monash svn repository cached, the script is entirely automatic but will
 display information relating to the tasks it is currently conducting.

4. Manually Merging Changes

 Its important to note that even though the Cray SVN repository for ccdb is meant
 to be a mirror of the Monash repository, it is not a perfect mirror. The Monash
 repository is laid out in a way to address the fact that guard is developed in
 such a way that it has two "seperate" entities. There is the open source component
 called "guard" and a private proprietary component called "guard-rd" which enables
 the core comparative functionality. But from a Cray viewpoint these two components
 can really be viewed as a single entity since we have a contractual agreement with
 Guardsoft for both pieces of software.

 It is recommended that the developer or maintainer use the merge_monash.sh script
 in order to synchronize any source changes, however if this is not possible or if
 the user needs to manually revert to a historical version, manual merging can take
 place.

 The merge_monash.sh script conducts the following steps automatically:

  1. Checks out a fresh copy of the Cray svn ccdb repository trunk.
  2. Reads the monash.rev file inside of the fresh copy to obtain the current Monash 
     revision number of the Cray source.
  3. Queries the current revision number of the Monash guard repository root as well
     as obtains the last changed revision number of each source trunk inside of the
     Monash guard repository.
  4. Compares the current Monash revision number from the monash.rev file to the current
     revision number obtained from the guard repository root to determine if a merge
     needs to take place.

  If a merge is needed:
  
   5. Creates a new tag within the Cray ccdb repository of the trunk to mark the state of the
      trunk before merging in the changes.
   6. Each of the last changed revision numbers obtained from the Monash guard repository source 
      trunks is compared to the current Monash revision number from the monash.rev file to 
      determine if a merge is needed for that particular trunk. If the last changed revision 
      number is greater than the current Monash revision of the Cray repository, we know a 
      merge needs to take place for that particular trunk.
   7. If a trunk merge needs to occur, the `svn merge` command is issued using the current Monash
      revision number from the monash.rev file as the "FROM" value, and the last changed
      revision number of the particular trunk as the "TO" value. This is merged into our
      local working copy that we initially checked out. Note that we only wish to do a diff
      of the changes for the trunk in question from the last time we merged in changes, to
      the last changed revision value. This is guarenteed to give us only the changes we
      desire to synchronize.
   8. After the merge is completed, the `autoreconf -i` command is run on each of the
      directories that recieved changes. This is to assit those who build the tool by
      removing the autosuite dependencies. (i.e. generate valid configure and Makefile.in files)
   9. Updates the monash.rev file to change the previous revision value to the current Monash
      revision number that was used for the current merge and change the current revision value
      to the current revision value of the Monash guard repository root. This keeps a history
      inside of the Cray repository of timeframes that each merge took place. (by comparing the
      historical states of the monash.rev file)
   10. A `svn ci` command is issued to push our changes to the local working copy to the Cray ccdb
      SVN repository.
   11. A new tag is created inside of the Cray ccdb repository trunk to mark the state of the trunk
      after the merge took place. This provides an easy mechanism to undo any erroneous merges to
      the Cray svn repository.
  12. (or 5.) Regardless of if a merge actually took place or not, the local working copy that we
      initially checked out from the Cray ccdb SVN repository is automatically removed as it is no
      longer needed.

 Any manual merge is possible, but it is recommended to follow a similar pattern as the one laid out
 above to conform with those who use the merge script. It is vital that after any manual merge is 
 conducted, the user edits the monash.rev file to reflect their changes. That way any user who uses
 the merge script can be certain that doing so will not break the Cray repository.
